            <ArticleTitle>Coronary artery calcium scoring is a better predictor of cardiac risk in subclinical hypothyroidism patients with low-risk Framingham score.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>756-762</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Overt hypothyroidism accelerates the cardiovascular disease. Subclinical hypothyroidism (SCH), being considered as a preclinical state, impacts on cardiovascular status is not clear.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study was aimed at assessing cardiac risk stratification by Framingham risk scoring (FRS) and coronary coronary artery calcium score (CACS) by noncontrast cardiac computed tomography in SCH.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Observational study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We enrolled thirty treatment-naive SCH patients (aged 30-60 years with no serious concurrent medical conditions), thirty euthyroid (age, sex, and body mass index-matched) controls, and ten healthy controls. All cases were evaluated for coronary artery calcium scoring and Framingham risk score.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STATISTICAL ANALYSIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Qualitative data were analyzed using the Chi-square test. In addition, demographics and CACS are summarized graphically or in a table.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">SCH cases had higher thyroglobulin, while there was a trend toward an increase in total cholesterol, low-density lipoprotein (LDL), very LDL, and decrease in HDL levels. All participants had low-risk FRS (10-year FRS &lt; 10%). The mean CACS in SCH was significantly higher than simple obese and healthy controls (47.17 vs. 2.67 vs. 0.00).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This study suggests that SCH is an independent risk factor for coronary artery disease in apparently healthy controls. The risk of occult coronary artery disease is increased in SCH cases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
